With `splines=true|spline`, the neato-family edge router (neato, fdp, and every engine that uses `neatosplines`) builds no obstacle for nodes of shape `star` or `cylinder`, so edges are routed straight through them. C builds a polygon obstacle for both shapes and routes around them. In the fuzz cases the missing obstacle also changes the triangulation, so the port routes a spline where C falls back to a line, or the other way round (`lib/pathplan/shortest.c:193: destination point not in any triangle` printed by only one side).

**Reproducer**

```dot
graph G {
layout=neato;
splines=true;
a [pos="0,0!"];
b [pos="3,0!"];
s [shape=star, pos="1.5,0!"];
c [pos="0,1.5!"];
d [pos="3,1.5!"];
y [shape=cylinder, pos="1.5,1.5!"];
a -- b;
c -- d;
}
```

```sh
GVBINDIR=<pango-free plugins> dot -Kneato -Tsvg repro.gv   # C Graphviz 16.1.0
graphviz-pure -Kneato -Tsvg repro.gv                              # port
```

**C output vs port**

```diff
@@ -3,10 +3,10 @@
